    <string name="trust_summary">How passwords are kept, and which routers work</string>
    <string name="trust_passwords">Your router passwords</string>
    <string name="trust_stored">Passwords are saved on this phone only, encrypted with a key kept in Android\'s secure key store. The key can\'t be copied off the phone.</string>
    <string name="trust_sent">A password is sent only to the router it belongs to, at the address you entered. RouterHub has no account and no server, and sends it nowhere else.</string>
    <string name="trust_export">Exported router files never contain passwords. A router imported from one asks for its password again.</string>
    <string name="trust_extensions">Router support comes from extensions, which run inside the app and receive the login of the routers that use them. The official extensions are open source and signed. Install others only from people you trust.</string>
    <string name="trust_permissions">The app needs network access: to reach your router, and to see which network you are on so it can find the router\'s address. It asks to show notifications only if you turn on new-device alerts, and it never asks for your location.</string>
    <string name="trust_no_ads">No ads, no analytics and no subscription.</string>
    <string name="trust_update_check">This version also asks GitHub whether a newer version is out. It sends nothing about you or your routers.</string>
    <string name="tested_routers">Tested routers</string>
    <string name="tested_intro">These were tried for real. Other models of the same firmware usually work too, but haven\'t been checked.</string>
    <string name="tested_openwrt_hardware">Status, clients, every settings page, Wi-Fi changes, blocking, reservations and port forwarding.</string>
    <string name="tested_openwrt_vm_name">OpenWrt in a virtual machine</string>
    <string name="tested_openwrt_vm">Status, every settings page, reservations and port forwarding. No Wi-Fi in a virtual machine.</string>
    <string name="tested_tenda">Status, clients, Wi-Fi switch, reboot and settings pages. Blocking from the clients list uses the router\'s MAC filter and hasn\'t been tried on the router yet.</string>
    <string name="tested_tplink">Status, DSL line, clients, reboot and settings pages. Blocking from the clients list isn\'t available yet: use the MAC filter page.</string>
